      "evidence_type": "published tag insertion at this exact site",
      "functional_impact_measured": "Surface expression confirmed by flow cytometry (the construct was designed for transient CD19 surface expression via mRNA transfection); functional assays beyond surface detection NOT MEASURED in this study.",
      "confidence": "high",
      "rationale": "L1 explicitly states the FLAG tag was inserted 'between amino acids 19 and 20' of CD19 (UniProt P15391), which is the junction between the signal peptide (1-19) and the mature extracellular N-terminus (Pro20). Python verification confirms residue 19 = R and residue 20 = P in the canonical sequence. The construct includes the TM domain (aa 292-313), confirming membrane anchoring. The tag sits 272 residues from the TM boundary, well within the extracellular span. Full-length membrane-anchored CD19 construct (aa 1-317, containing ECD and TM domain) expressed transiently via mRNA transfection in mammalian cells. The FLAG tag was inserted between residues 19 and 20 (after the native signal peptide), flanked by G4S and (G4S)2 linkers, with the native CD19 sequence resuming at Pro20.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "pmid": 38155191,
          "citation": "PMID 38155191",
          "claim": "Transgenes for in vitro transcription and subsequent transient CD19 surface expression were designed by cloning a T7 promoter upstream of the native signal peptide of CD19 (Uniprot P15391 ; amino acids 1\u201319), followed by a linker sequence (G 4 S), a Flag-tag, a linker sequence (G 4 S) 2 and a truncated sequence of the native CD19 (Uniprot P15391 ; amino acids 20\u2013317) containing the extracellular domains and the transmembrane domain (sequence shown in the Supplementary Table 1 )."
        }
      ],
